  "narrative": "These twelve follow-up analyses mostly confirm that the already accepted large associations are robust to common co-mutational contexts, rather than revealing new large effects. For PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency, H34 and H35 show that the association is essentially unchanged in adenocarcinoma and remains robust when TP53-loss lines are excluded: both signed estimates are roughly +0.27 to +0.29 with intervals far from zero and far beyond the 10% difference benchmark. The raw interpretation remains that PTEN-loss lines have more negative STAT3 dependency scores, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. This is not a new causal claim, but it materially strengthens the dataset-specific evidence for the accepted PTEN-loss association. H36 found no evidence that lineage subtype modifies the PTEN-loss effect; the interaction point estimate is near zero, but the interval is wide enough that a moderate interaction cannot be excluded, so I leave it unresolved and deferred rather than claiming absence. H37 was the only interaction test with a nontrivial point estimate, but it is in the direction opposite to the hypothesized negative interaction: the signed estimate is negative, implying PTEN loss may be less strongly associated with STAT3 dependency in TP53-loss lines. Because the interval is wide and the exposed PTEN-loss/TP53-loss cell is small, I do not accept it as decisive; I reject the original hypothesized direction and close it without validation because it does not overturn the main PTEN-loss finding and is not strong enough to justify spending an independent validation. H38 and H39 showed no evidence of PIK3CA- or KRAS-mutation modification of the PTEN-loss STAT3 association; intervals are broad and centered near zero, so I record them as unresolved but deferred, not as evidence for meaningful context dependence. H40 rejects PIK3CA mutation as a driver of stronger STAT3 dependency among PTEN-wild-type lines: the effect is tiny and the interval excludes meaningful magnitude. For the reversed KEAP1/NFE2L2 association, H41 shows no meaningful modification by STK11-loss status, and H42 shows that the large KEAP1-loss association is essentially intact in STK11-wild-type lines. This makes it unlikely that the accepted KEAP1/NFE2L2 result is carried by STK11-loss co-occurrence. H43 and H44 likewise show that the very large SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 and MSI-high/WRN associations persist in TP53-wild-type lines with nearly unchanged huge effects. These are strong dataset-specific robustness results, not causal or external-generalization claims. H45 found only a very small EGFR-amplification association in KRAS-wild-type lines, with an interval entirely below any meaningful 10% effect range and compatible with the null; this closes the idea that KRAS co-occurrence was masking a scientifically meaningful EGFR-amplification effect. I did not request validation because the newly accepted items are confirmatory robustness checks of effects already supported by very tight intervals, and the only suggestive interaction trend, H37, is underpowered and not decisive enough to justify validation.",
  "research_notes": "Round 3 robustness update. Accepted dataset-specific associations now include: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-H -> stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency. The new batch strongly supports robustness: PTEN-loss effect in STAT3 is retained in adenocarcinoma and TP53-wild-type lines; KEAP1-loss effect in NFE2L2 is retained in STK11-wild-type lines; SMARCA4/SMARCA2 and MSI/WRN are retained in TP53-wild-type lines. Context interactions for PTEN-loss are mostly null: no support for lineage-subtype, TP53-loss, PIK3CA-mutation, or KRAS-mutation modification of the hypothesized direction. H37 shows a possible opposite-direction attenuation in TP53-loss lines but is underpowered and rejected for the original claim. PIK3CA mutation in PTEN-wild-type lines does not meaningfully increase STAT3 dependency. EGFR amplification in KRAS-wild-type lines is small and closed. No validation requested: H14/PTEN already independently validated; new accepted claims are robust confirmations; H37 is not decisive enough. Remaining low-priority deferred items include TMED10/ERBB2, MDM2/TP53 adenocarcinoma, RIT1/BRAF bypass, EGFR/ERBB2/KRAS bypass probes, MYC/KIF18A, RB1/POLR2A, PIK3CA/STAT3 global effect, and broad interaction refinements if a future validation or larger signal justifies them."
